To generate the appropriate assert statement for the given comment, we need to carefully interpret the conditions described:

1. The index is between 0 and the size of the list inclusive.
2. For every valid index from this index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null and the old element at this index is null, or the element at the next position equals the old element at this index.

Given these conditions, we can translate them into an assert statement using the provided syntax rules.

Here's the assert statement in a `<code></code>` block:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i + 1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i + 1) == \old(this.get(i));</code>

This assert statement ensures that for every valid index `i` starting from `index` up to the old size of the list, the element at the next position (`i + 1`) is either null when the old element at `i` is null, or the element at `i + 1` equals the old element at `i`.
